      },
      {
        "factor": "Market expectations of regime-change risk",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Investor uncertainty about regime change adds ambiguity and can dampen the certainty of a stable 2/3 outcome."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Historical bias toward two-thirds under current rules",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "strong",
        "rationale": "Systemic bias tends to dampen chances of a 2/3, especially with fragmentation and shifting coalitions."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis_rationale": "Starting from the 0.56 historical anchor, I weighed upside and downside forces. Structural bias and fragmentation pull the probability down, while potential opposition unity and turnout upsides pull it up. The net result is a modestly balanced expectation with substantial uncertainty, yielding a final probability around 0.50 and a 0.40–0.60 range to reflect calibration needs and turnout/coalition volatility."
  }
}
